---
import { SITE, NAV_LINKS } from '../../lib/site';

/**
 * Persistent top bar, every route (docs/user-flows.md "Navigation
 * convention").
 *
 * AMENDED 2026-09-10: the full-screen menu docket (Phase 3c) is retired —
 * Gary's call, after seeing affaanmustafa.com's plain always-visible nav
 * links and preferring that structure. Removed with it: MenuPanel.astro
 * entirely, and the focus-trap/inert/open-close script that only existed
 * to manage that overlay. What's kept: the sticky-on-scroll behavior and
 * the wordmark's scroll-to-top click handler (2026-09-09), neither of
 * which had anything to do with the menu.
 *
 * Sticky no longer needs the html.js gating the menu required. That
 * gating existed only because the menu's entire link list used to render
 * unconditionally INSIDE this header (the no-JS fallback), making it
 * ~900px tall — pinning that over the viewport for a no-JS visitor would
 * have hidden real content. A plain link row has no such height problem
 * in either state, and `position: sticky` is native CSS that needs no
 * JavaScript to begin with. Sticky is now a plain, ungated class.
 *
 * AMENDED 2026-09-10 (b): the wordmark + 4 links used to share one
 * `justify-between` row, which at phone widths (≤~400px) forced the nav
 * links to wrap mid-list into a cramped two-line stack fighting the
 * wordmark for space — a real bug, caught by inspecting the actual mobile
 * render, not a deliberate density choice. Below `sm` (40rem, the same
 * breakpoint @utility axis already collapses at) the row stacks instead:
 * wordmark first, link row second, each with room to lay out on its own.
 *
 * AMENDED 2026-09-10 (c): (b) wasn't enough — down at genuinely narrow
 * widths (~320-360px, this project's own stated test floor,
 * PROJECT_FACTS.md) the wordmark was still borrowing `text-placard`, the
 * SAME clamp the homepage hero uses, so it wrapped to two lines and ate
 * most of the header on its own; the four links then wrapped unevenly
 * into a ragged 3-line stack below it. Two real fixes: the wordmark is an
 * identity mark, not a hero — it now sits at the fixed `text-lede` size
 * so it never competes with page headings and reliably stays on one line
 * down to 320px. And below `sm` the link row becomes a clean one-per-line
 * vertical list instead of an uncontrolled wrap — deliberate stacking
 * reads as intentional; wrap-induced stacking reads as broken.
 */
---

<header class="axis bg-stock-200 max-w-stock sticky top-0 z-40 mx-auto">
  <div
    class="py-gutter gap-gutter flex flex-col items-start sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between"
  >
    <a href="/" data-wordmark class="stencil text-lede text-ink uppercase">
      {SITE.name}
    </a>
    <nav
      class="gap-gutter flex flex-col items-start sm:flex-row sm:flex-wrap sm:items-center"
      aria-label="Site"
    >
      {
        NAV_LINKS.map((link) => (
          <a href={link.href} class="stencil text-mark uppercase hover:underline">
            {link.label}
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </nav>
  </div>
  <div class="hazard-rule"></div>
</header>

<script>
  // Wordmark: scroll-to-top when already home, real navigation otherwise.
  // The CSS `scroll-behavior: auto` reduced-motion override in global.css
  // does NOT catch this — an explicit `behavior: 'smooth'` in a JS call
  // wins over that CSS property, so reduced motion has to be checked here
  // directly, not assumed to be handled globally.
  const wordmark = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-wordmark]');

  wordmark?.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
    if (window.location.pathname !== '/') return;
    event.preventDefault();
    const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: reduceMotion ? 'auto' : 'smooth' });
  });
</script>
